ABSTRACT Despite the growth and interest in information processing research, understanding the supporting role of information systems (IS) has been limited. While cognitive processing of information has been examined in learning environments with traditional learning tasks, the investigation of cognitive load within complex simulated IS learning environments has received less attention. Traditional measurement allows for a broad user evaluation of the ISs and actual usage from a holistic perspective; however, detailed synchronous evaluation of cognitive load during the usage of the IS may allow for more accurate assessment of how system features influence cognitive load and subsequent performance outcomes. Therefore, this research attempts to integrate traditional subjective and physiological measurements to examine cognitive load within a dynamic simulated IS learning environment. This research study focuses on how subjective and objective physiological (galvanic skin response (GSR), heart rate variability (HRV), and electroencephalography (EEG) measures of cognitive load compare in simulated IS training environments. Keywords Cognitive Load, NeuroIS physiological tools, training, IS learning. INTRODUCTION Recent directions in research have led to the emergent evaluation of theories and foundations with objective physiological and neurological measurement (Dimoka, Pavlou & Davis, 2011). This thrust has been an attempt to validate and evaluate methods of collecting data on the physiological changes that occur in an individual during various phenomena. Typically, empirical evidence of theoretical testing has been evaluated with self-report scale-based measurement items that attempt to capture an unobservable behavior. Due to the latency characteristics, post-hoc evaluat…

[8] Age-Friendly User Interfaces in Production: An Empirical Study on How Human-Centered Design Can Improve Cognitive Ergonomics and Counteract Demographic Problems by Andreas Dörner, Marek Bures, Michal Simon, Gerald Pirkl :: SSRN (2025-06-17T03:10:12.000Z)
URL: https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=5298807
Industrial companies face ongoing challenges, including the shift toward human-centered production and an ageing workforce. Both are underexplored in research, particularly in combination. This article addresses these gaps by examining the impact of information load on MES user interfaces on cognitive ergonomics, with a focus on age-related differences. An experiment was conducted with 64 participants performing an assembly task. 32 used a standard interface, and 32 used a lean-optimized version. The subjects have been clustered into two groups, over and under 45 years of age. Cognitive load was measured using NASA-TLX, galvanic skin conductance and heart rate. A statistical analysis revealed that lean and human-centered design significantly lowered cognitive load, enhancing ergonomic quality of the user interface. Importantly, results showed that older participants experienced greater cognitive strain from higher information loads compared to younger counterparts. These findings highlight the importance of age-friendly design in the era of Industry 5.0. The study demonstrates that reducing information overload is critical for supporting older workers, especially significant in the subjective measurement, reinforcing the relevance of inclusive, age-sensitive design in industrial settings.
