The mechanism is simple: pre-launch classroom training is forgotten quickly. Adult learners retain a fraction of what they hear if they do not apply it within days. By the time go-live happens and users finally need the workflow, most of the lecture content is gone. What is left is muscle memory from the old system. Without structured reinforcement after launch, that muscle memory wins.

There is a sharper version of this problem from the implementation community: if readiness is defined as training attendance rather than demonstrated capability, go-live exposes the gap immediately. People who sat through a session are not the same as people who can do the job. The 30-60-90 plan is built around capability, not attendance.
