The late nineteen-eighties writings of Marilyn Strathern did not present the sole seminal contribution to the development of the theoretical movement subsequently christened the New Melanesian Ethnography. Lisette Josephides in her 1991 review article applied the label to four monographs published in 1987-1988: Fredrik Barth's Cosmologies in the making, Jadran Mimica's Intimations of infinity, James Weiner's The heart of the pearlshell, and Strathern's The gender of the gift (Josephides 1991 ). However, it was the latter volume that (arguably) was the most radical in its epistemology, received the biggest share of traction and critical comment, and later came to define NME to the extent of Strathern's notion of "dividual" becoming essentially synonymous with it.

