[3] Comprehensive investigation of aggregative and sedimentation stability assessment methods for diesel fuels with enhanced low-temperature properties - ScienceDirect (2026-03-28T14:42:55.000Z)
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0016236126009956
Diesel fuels can be fundamentally classified into four grades based on their low-temperature properties: summer (DFS), off-season or interseason for spring and autumn periods (DFI), winter (DFW), and arctic (DFA). The differences in properties among these diesel fuel grades are primarily determined by their specific application conditions, particularly the ambient temperature. When fuel is used in conditions too cold for a given grade, there is a high risk of crystallization of linear paraffinic hydrocarbons, clogging of fuel filters, and disruption of the fuel supply system. Winter and especially arctic diesel fuels have more stringent requirements for low-temperature stability to ensure their safe storage, transportation, and use. Main production of DFW and DFA is concentrated in Europe and North America, due to the climatic characteristics of these regions [4], [5].

Traditionally, the low-temperature properties of diesel fuel are defined by three quantitative indicators, which may be specified in standards: cloud point (CP), cold filter plugging point (CFPP), and pour point (PP).

CP characterizes the temperature at which the crystallization of paraffins present in diesel fuel begins, resulting in the fuel losing its transparency. Typically, the cloud point value for diesel fuel is higher than other low-temperature indicators.

CFPP characterizes the temperature at which diesel fuel takes longer than 60 s to flow through a standard filter. The cold filter plugging point is typically lower than the cloud point because it is assumed that, by the time CFPP is reached, paraffins in the fuel have not only crystallized but have also grown to a size that significantly impedes flow through the fuel system. It should be noted that the CFPP value is influenced not only by th…

