In recent years, the quality of synthetic media has significantly improved. Artificial intelligence (AI)-based generation, editing, or style transfers are so real to be detected with the naked eye. This research covers the domain of AI -generated fake imagery, which happens when someone adopts another's identity. This transfer includes expression, emotion, hair, and makeup, but it also extends beyond these specific styles. We propose a dataset to power deep-fake forensics. The generated GHOST-DF dataset contains 97212 males and 65715 females, synthetically swapped facial images. The employed method for generation is the Generative High-fidelity One Shot Transfer (GHOST). The target dataset contains 15270 and 14731 real images of males and females extracted from CelebAMask-HQ. A state-of-the-art (SOTA) deep learning-based universal deepfake detector is used to validate the proposed dataset. On running inference, a signifi-cant drop in the performance of the employed SOTA framework, i.e., Universal Fake Detector, to 64.6% has been observed. Using transfer learning, we fine-tuned the detector model and achieved an overall performance improvement of 97.9 % for our introduced dataset. Furthermore, the fine-tuned model is tested on other fake datasets to establish its generalization ability. The project page is https:/hdthub.com/anushka0415/GHOST-DF.
