2 Background
This section briefly introduces the background and its related works, structured around three focal points. First, we briefly introduce deepfake generation techniques, including face-swapping (FS), face-reenactment (FR), entire face synthesis (EFS), and face editing (FE). We consider both academic works and popular software online.
Second, we delve into deepfake detection, including face forgery detection and AIGC detection.
Last, we review existing deepfake datasets and benchmarks, underscoring the need for diverse and comprehensive datasets for next-generation deepfake detection.
2.1 Deepfake Generation.
Based on previous survey [54], deepfake techniques can be typically classified into four types: face-swapping (FS), face-reenactment (FR), entire face synthesis (EFS), and face editing (FE).
(1) Face-swapping: This paper classifies the face-swapping technique into two domains: DF-family and FS-family.
(i) DF-family involves creating a mask around the facial region (some even include the neck [50]) and blending the generated deepfake face back into the background image using that mask. Most existing and famous face forgery datasets, such as FF-DF [67], Celeb-DF [47] and DFDC [20], belong to this line.
(ii) FS-family methods represent another significant category in face-swapping deepfake generation.
These methods typically involve the use of an identity-background encoder. It disentangles a face image’s identity and background information during encoding. Notably, these methods directly generate all content, even the background. Much recent face-swapping research works [90; 13] and popular software (e.g.e.g., Roop [65]) are within this line.
(2) Face-reenactment: Generally, this technique can be used to modify source faces, imitating the actions or expressions of another face.
Differing from face-swapping, face-reenactment techniques are rarely considered in existing datasets.
Two commonly used reenactment-based forgeries are Face2Face [77] and NeuralTextures [76].
These two forgeries are implemented in the FF++ dataset.
Face2Face employs pairs of original and target faces, using key facial points to generate varied expressions, while NeuralTexture uses rendered images from a 3D face model to migrate expressions.
Although these forgeries achieve more realistic visual synthetic results compared to their face-swapping counterparts in FF++,
Due to the amazingly rapid development of the AIGC technologies (e.g.e.g., Digital Human), these relatively old-fashioned methods cannot represent the modern’ SoTA reenactment methods.
Our DF40 implements 13 face-reenactment methods in total, including the classical animation [72; 73], SoTA’s audio-based driven methods [97; 60], image-based driven methods [86; 31; 7]. We also include the well-known best face generation technique, HeyGen [28], in our dataset for evaluation.
(3) Entire Face Synthesis: This technique can be generally treated as "Face AIGC." With the rapid development of AIGCs, this technique has achieved remarkably notable improvement. The two widely used technologies to generate synthesis faces are GAN (e.g.e.g., StarGAN [14]) and Diffusion models (e.g.e.g., StableDiffusion [64]).
(4) Face Editing: This technique aims to modify the facial attributes (e.g.e.g., age and gender) of the given face images. Most of these works utilize the latent code of StyleGAN [39] to perform editing during GAN inversion. In our DF40 dataset, we also consider the diffusion-based face editing method [32].
2.2 Deepfake Detection
Since deepfake detection can be broadly classified into face forgery detection and (general) AIGC detection (not limited to face), we will briefly introduce the typical works of each sub-field.
(1) Face Forgery Detection.
Earlier deepfake detection techniques primarily relied on traditional digital image processing methods. These approaches utilized natural video characteristics such as phoneme-viseme mismatches [4], depth of field [34], color components [43], and head poses [96].
However, with the advent of deep learning, the focus has now shifted towards learning-based detectors due to their superior feature extraction capabilities.
Following the recent deepfake benchmark [94], Most existing deepfake detectors can be broadly divided into naive, spatial, and frequency.
(i) The naive detector employs CNNs to perform binary classification that distinguishes deepfake content from authentic data. Numerous CNN-based binary classifiers have been proposed, e.g.e.g., MesoNet [2] and Xception [67];
(ii) Spatial detector delves deeper into specific representation such as forgery region location [56], disentanglement learning [93], image reconstruction [8], erasing technology [79], image blending [70; 45; 11], e​t​cetc;
(iii) The frequency detector addresses this limitation by focusing on the frequency domain for forgery detection.
SPSL [48] and SRM [52] utilize phase spectrum analysis and high-frequency noises, respectively.
F3Net [61] proposes using learnable filters for adaptive mining of frequency forgery clues using frequency-aware image decomposition.
In our work, we further make another new type: blending-based detectors due to the distinct training paradigm. These methods specifically focus on the detection of blending artifacts [46; 45; 11], generating forged images during training in a self-supervised manner to boost detector generalization.
These techniques aim to extract more comprehensive and discriminative features by leveraging various aspects of the input data.
(2) AIGC Detection.
As generative models produce increasingly realistic images, the importance of AIGC detection has grown. In earlier studies, AutoGAN [98] revealed the presence of unique artifacts in GAN-generated images, which can be observed periodically in the images’ frequency spectrum. Wang et al. [82] suggested that a classifier trained on one CNN generator, with suitable training data and data augmentation, can generalize effectively to other unseen generators.
However, these approaches have exhibited limited generalization capabilities when applied to images generated by diffusion models. Recently, DIRE [87] employed the error between an input image and its reconstruction using a pre-trained diffusion model to detect fake images. Nonetheless, this method is specifically designed for diffusion models and cannot be directly applied to detecting GAN-based models.
2.3 Existing Deepfake Datasets.
Most earlier public deepfake datasets published before 2021 [41; 96; 21; 47; 67; 17]
generate deepfakes using only the face-swapping technique within "DF-family."
Also, these datasets contain only single or no more than 4 specific manipulation approaches.
Notably, DFDC [20] implements 7 deepfake approaches (most are DF-family face-swapping) and extends the data scale to the million-level.
After that, ForgeryNet [27] applies 15 methods to create forgery data with a million-level data scale.
However, with the advent of both AIGCs and diffusion-based generators, producing realistic content has become increasingly popular and widely seen on social media.
Consequently, it is uncertain whether a detector trained on older forgery methods can generalize to today’s SoTA deepfakes, e.g.e.g., diffusion-generated content.
In response, many recent works [36; 74] have begun to focus on current generative models.
A very recent study, DiffusionDeepfake [6], proposes a diffusion-specific dataset containing two mainstream state-of-the-art diffusion generators: Stable-Diffusion [64] and MidJourney [53].
Furthermore, to detect these AIGC-generated images (not limited to face), GenImage [102] introduces a million-scale dataset created by 8 different generative models (including both GAN and Diffusion).
However, it is crucial to note that [6] and [102] are still within only the AIGC scope and may not guarantee successful detection of other deepfakes, e.g.e.g., face-swapping.
A more comprehensive comparison of existing datasets can be referred to Tab. 1.
