The rapid advancement of deepfake generation techniques has created a need for advanced detection models, with convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and transformer-based methods demonstrating great results on benchmark datasets including FaceForensics++. These models have been able to achieve almost perfect accuracy in controlled settings. More importantly, many current deepfake detection services work in centralized cloud-based systems, this requires users to upload possibly sensitive digital media for analysis, which poses significant privacy risks. To address these concerns, this study investigates the application of Fully Homomorphic Encryption (FHE) as a privacy-preserving alternative, implemented using Concrete ML developed by Zama. Unlike differential privacy and secure multi-party computation, which suffer from accuracy degradation or coordination overhead, FHE enables inference directly on encrypted data without requiring decryption, thus preserving both confidentiality and model utility. This work integrates Discrete Cosine Transform (DCT)-based preprocessing, Quantization Aware Training (QAT), and Residual Network architectures into a homomorphic inference pipeline. Experimental results demonstrate substantial reductions in homomorphic operations and latency through frequency-domain optimization, while maintaining statistically equivalent accuracy under simulated and encrypted execution modes. Collectively, this research establishes the technical feasibility and practical viability of deploying privacy-preserving deepfake detection systems using homomorphic encryption.

