CNN-based detectors (7 methods) include classical convolutional architectures such as ResNet [50] and EfficientNet [8], as well as specialized designs such as PatchCraft [9] for patch-level texture analysis. The AIDE family [10] enables controlled analysis of training data effects, with variants trained on ProGAN, GenImage, and Stable Diffusion v1.4 using the same underlying architecture.
Transformer-based detectors (6 methods) leverage attention mechanisms for modeling long-range dependencies. The DRCT family [12] includes both CLIP-ViT and ConvNeXt backbones trained on different Stable Diffusion versions, enabling systematic comparison of architectural and training data effects. Effort[43] builds on a foundation vision model by projecting images into an orthogonal forgery subspace learned from diverse generative sources, while ForgeLens [44] employs CLIP-ViT representations with weakly supervised guidance, demonstrating data-efficient detection using only a small fraction of standard training data.
Frequency-domain detectors (5 methods) from the AIGCDetectionBench suite [22] analyze spectral characteristics using DCT transforms, Gram matrices, Laplacian gradients, and multi-frequency fusion strategies.
Ensemble and hybrid detectors (3 methods) combine multiple models or employ mixture-of-experts designs. Community-Forensics [16] aggregates multiple pre-trained detectors; unless otherwise stated, we report results using the primary released model and exclude auxiliary versions from aggregate statistics. SAFE [17] dynamically selects specialized detectors based on input characteristics, while Forensic-MoE [51] routes inputs to expert subnetworks via a mixture-of-experts architecture.
This diverse selection enables systematic comparison of architectural paradigms, training strategies, and ensemble approaches under identical zero-shot evaluation conditions.
