The ergonomic assessment of haptic feedback systems versus traditional mechanical controls reveals several critical challenges that impact user comfort, performance, and long-term health outcomes. Traditional controls, such as physical buttons, knobs, and switches, have established ergonomic profiles based on decades of research and refinement. However, the transition to haptic interfaces introduces new ergonomic considerations that require comprehensive evaluation. Physical fatigue represents a primary concern in haptic systems. Unlike traditional controls that provide inherent mechanical resistance and tactile landmarks, haptic interfaces often require users to maintain sustained contact with flat touchscreen surfaces or force-feedback devices. This continuous contact without natural rest positions can lead to increased muscle tension in fingers, hands, and forearms. The absence of physical detents and mechanical stops forces users to rely more heavily on visual feedback, reducing the kinesthetic awareness that traditional controls naturally provide. Cognitive load differences between the two control paradigms present another significant challenge. Traditional mechanical controls offer immediate tactile confirmation through physical movement and resistance, allowing users to operate them with minimal visual attention. Haptic systems, despite sophisticated vibrotactile feedback, often require users to divide attention between the control surface and visual displays to confirm input registration. This increased cognitive demand can lead to mental fatigue, particularly in high-workload environments such as automotive or industrial applications. Precision and accuracy issues emerge when comparing fine motor control requirements. Traditional controls typically feature dis…

