articleThe anonymity paradox: Navigating face-saving and credibility in Chinese EFL peer feedback practices
...
In Chinese higher education, peer feedback implementation encounters distinctive cultural tensions rooted in Confucian educational traditions that emphasize teacher authority (shīdào zūnyán), hierarchical social relationships, examination-oriented achievement, and individual competitive performance rather than collaborative learning (Lee & Mao, 2024; Wang et al., 2024). Students typically perceive instructors as unquestioned knowledge authorities and sole legitimate evaluators, viewing writing as an individual cognitive activity rather than a socially constructed practice. Although recent educational reforms have advocated for more communicative and student-centered pedagogies, practical implementation frequently confronts resistance grounded in deeply ingrained cultural values such as face-saving (miànzi), interpersonal harmony (héxié), modesty (qiānxū), and respect for status hierarchies. These cultural orientations generate complex affective, relational, and evaluative dynamics that can either enable or constrain authentic peer interaction during feedback exchanges.

Despite expanding research on peer feedback within EFL settings, existing scholarship has predominantly concentrated on procedural implementation, effectiveness comparisons between peer and teacher feedback, or general attitudinal surveys, without sufficiently interrogating the underlying sociocultural mechanisms that shape students' engagement with peer review practices (Chien, 2022). This gap is particularly acute in collectivist, high power-distance Confucian-heritage cultures such as China, where interpersonal relationships (guānxi), emotional considerations, perceived competence hierarchi…

