Parents’ Experiences of Home–School Collaboration via the Digital Platform Wilma
Authors
Riikka Sirkko
University of Oulu, Finland
Ninnu Kotilainen
University of Oulu, Finland
Riikka Mononen
University of Oulu, Finland
DOI:
https://doi.org/10.23865/nse.v45.6972
Abstract
This study investigated parents’ experiences of home–school collaboration in grades 1–9 via the digital platform Wilma, comparing the experiences between primary and lower secondary education. Parents (N = 558; 32.6% of whom were parents of pupils receiving special educational support) answered an online questionnaire. Pupils receiving special educational support got more daily Wilma markings than expected by chance alone. Parents, regardless of support and school level, found the markings rather beneficial. Wilma was perceived as a good technical tool, but as an ambiguous pedagogical tool that lacked common practices. The need for common guidelines for using Wilma is discussed.
