[3] Do peer assessment ratings reflect individual contributions? Validity evidence from multicultural student teams (2026-05-14T00:00:00.000Z)
URL: https://exa.ai/library/publication/cswqvrtn2tq
Peer assessment is widely used in team-based higher education; however, its validity as an indicator of individual contributions remains contested, particularly in culturally diverse teams. This study examines the criterion-related validity of peer ratings in an eight-week global virtual classroom program (January–April 2026) involving 74 students in nine mixed-nationality teams from Germany and Mexico. Using a multi-source validation design, peer ratings were compared with three independent indicators of contribution: weekly behavioural participation logs, end-of-program reflection depth, and battle-reflection cognitive engagement during cross-cultural negotiation simulations. The analysis also tests whether peer scores vary systematically by the nationality of the evaluator. Peer ratings captured visible behavioural participation under measurement-equivalent conditions (r = 0.66, k = 8), but showed minimal alignment with less observable cognitive contributions (reflection depth r = 0.14; engagement r = −0.28). Peer ratings also differed significantly by evaluator nationality (d = −0.43, p < 0.001), a gap not observed in independent post-program outcomes, indicating a rater-calibration effect. These findings suggest that peer assessment provides partial evidence of observable contributions and is most valid when embedded in a multi-source, equity-aware evaluation framework.

