conventionally grouped under the label “Epic Cycle”, the 
Cypria occupies a structurally privileged and methodologically vexing posi 
tion. Structurally, it narrates the long prehistory of the Trojan War—its remote 
divine “causes”, the assembling of the expedition, and initial phase of the 
campaign—only to deliver, at its terminus, a handover to the Iliad so tight that 
it has repeatedly invited the language of “prequel” or “introduction” to Homer. 
Methodologically, however, the actual poem is lost: what we possess are (i) a 
relatively detailed prose plot-summary transmitted under Proclus’ name, and 
(ii) a heterogeneous collection of testimonia and fragments quoted for anti 
quarian, grammatical, or exegetical purposes. Any reading of the Cypria must 
therefore be a composite act: reconstruction of narrative sequence; evaluation 
