This paper explores the concept of motif transference within Homeric poetry, framing it as an active narratological tool. By examining the cyclical nature of myth and epic and how Homer’s work interacts with pre-Homeric traditions, it argues for a nuanced understanding of the Iliad and Odyssey as both derivative and innovative. The neoanalytical approach is employed to elucidate how earlier traditions inform Homeric texts and how these influences are reflected in the narrative structure and motifs.
