The presence of major high-risk features should prompt obtaining an ECG. Such features include:

- Signs of acute ischemia or ischemic heart disease or cardiomyopathy 
- Presence of Mobitz II second- and third-degree atrioventricular (AV) block, bundle branch block, intraventricular conduction disturbances, ventricular hypertrophy, sustained and nonsustained ventricular tachycardia 
- Presence of type 1 Brugada pattern (ST-elevation in V1-V3) or the consistent presence of a QTc interval longer than 460 milliseconds in repeated 12-lead ECGs, suggesting long QT syndrome 
- Slow atrial fibrillation (AF) (HR < 40 beats/minute) 
- Persistent sinus bradycardia (HR < 40 beats/minute) or repetitive sinoatrial block or sinus pauses (>3 seconds) when awake and in the absence of physical training 
- Pacemaker or implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD) dysfunction 

Characteristics considered minor high risk (only if the patient's history is consistent with arrhythmogenic syncope) include:

- Presence of Mobitz I second-degree AV block, AV block I with a markedly prolonged PR interval, asymptomatic bradycardia (HR of 40-50 beats/minute), paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia or AF 
- Presence of a preexcited QRS complex or a short QTc interval (< 340 millisecods) 
