- GitHub issues each workflow run a short-lived OIDC token. AWS exchanges it for temporary credentials. No secret is stored anywhere.
- The `sub` claim condition is the entire security boundary. Get it wrong and the role becomes assumable by workflows you do not control.
- Omitting the `sub` condition while keeping only `aud` allows any GitHub Actions workflow on GitHub to assume your role. Every GitHub OIDC token carries the same audience.
- Compute the provider thumbprint from the issuer rather than hardcoding one, so the configuration survives certificate rotation.
...
The starting position is an `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` and `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` stored as GitHub secrets. That credential does not expire, exists in at least two places from the moment it is created, and appears in whatever config stores, logs and screenshots it passes through on the way. It maps to OWASP NHI7 Long-Lived Secrets and, once it leaks, NHI2 Secret Leakage.

Rotating it on a schedule shortens the window in which a leak is useful. It does not remove the secret, the distribution problem, or the difficulty of enumerating everywhere it has been copied. Federation removes the stored credential outright: the workflow proves what it is to an issuer AWS already trusts, and receives credentials that expire in roughly an hour.
...
