[3] How to Eliminate Long-Lived Cloud Keys in CI/CD Using OIDC | Reclear (2026-06-06T06:28:40.000Z)
URL: https://reclear.io/blog/oidc-for-cicd-eliminate-long-lived-cloud-keys
- Long-lived `AKIA` keys in GitHub Secrets are standing privilege: every workflow that reads the secret inherits cloud access until someone rotates keys.
- OIDC federation issues short-lived STS credentials bound to repository, branch, or environment claims in the JWT `sub` and `aud` fields.
- Configure the GitHub OIDC provider once per AWS account, scope an IAM role trust policy tightly, and set `permissions: id-token: write` in the workflow.
- Use separate roles for staging and production; bind production to GitHub Environments with required reviewers.
- Keep fork PR workflows off deploy roles; split read-only CI roles from deploy roles.

If your deployment pipeline authenticates to AWS with access keys stored in GitHub Secrets, you are carrying credentials that outlive every job. Those keys are often over-permissioned, rotated manually (if ever), and visible to every workflow that can read the secret. When a workflow is compromised through a poisoned dependency, a typosquatted action, or a fork PR exfiltration trick, the blast radius is your cloud account until someone notices.

OpenID Connect (OIDC) federation changes the credential shape. CI jobs receive short-lived tokens minted for a specific repository, branch, and environment, bound to an IAM role with least privilege. No static keys in GitHub. No shared deploy user in a password manager.

This guide is for platform and DevSecOps engineers running GitHub Actions against AWS. The same pattern generalizes to GCP Workload Identity and Azure Federated Credentials. You will configure an IAM OIDC identity provider, a trust-scoped IAM role, and a deploy workflow that assumes the role without secrets.
