How Sekoia built Aviary: a Kubernetes canary deployment controller for Kafka-based microservices with automated rollback and zero codebase changes required.

- Sekoia processes ~1 billion client events per day; even seconds of detection pipeline downtime are unacceptable, making safe deployment of ~80 microservices on Kubernetes a critical operational challenge.
- Aviary is Sekoia's open-source canary deployment controller for Kubernetes: it transparently creates -primary and -canary deployments, progressively scales them inversely, and monitors Prometheus/Kubernetes metrics at each step to decide promotion or rollback.
- Aviary activates canary rollouts only on Docker image changes (not CPU/RAM/metadata updates), requires no changes to existing Kubernetes deployment manifests or CI/CD pipelines, and adds no heavy dependencies to the cluster.
- Unlike HTTP-centric solutions (Gloo, Flagger) or ecosystem-requiring tools (Argo Rollouts), Aviary is designed for Kafka-based workers and generic Kubernetes deployments without session pinning or traffic-routing infrastructure.
- Per-service configuration covers break-point percentage, canary instance increment per step, maximum tolerated metric failures, warm-up delay, and metric thresholds; operators can cancel in-progress deployments or bypass Aviary for hotfixes.
