The pipeline runs on every push to `main`:

| Stage | Job | Mode | Description |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `build` | `build` | Auto | Builds the container image and pushes to the project's container registry |
| `test` | `test`, SAST, etc. | Auto | Runs tests, code quality, container scanning, and secret detection |
| `staging` | `staging` | Auto | Deploys to the staging environment via Helm |
| `canary` | `canary` | Auto | Deploys a canary Helm release (`production-canary`) alongside the stable `production` release |
| `production` | `rollout 10%` | Manual | Begins incremental production rollout at 10% |
| `production` | `rollout 25%` | Manual | Increases rollout to 25% |
| `production` | `rollout 50%` | Manual | Increases rollout to 50% |
| `production` | `rollout 100%` | Manual | Completes the full production rollout |
...
Auto-DevOps uses the auto-deploy-app Helm chart to manage deployments:

1. Canary release (`production-canary`): A separate Helm release deployed during the `canary` stage with a subset of replicas to validate the new version.
2. Stable release (`production`): The main Helm release that serves production traffic, updated incrementally during the rollout stages.
3. The canary release runs alongside the stable release, allowing you to validate the new version before committing to a full rollout.
4. Aborting a canary is safe since the stable release remains untouched until the rollout stages.

