Climate change is one of the planet’s most urgent challenges. Decarbonization helps address it by reducing greenhouse gas emissions across the global economy. In many sectors, this includes shifting away from fossil fuels like coal, oil and natural gas toward lower-emission energy sources. However, energy transition is only one piece of the decarbonization puzzle. In food and agriculture, a significant share of emissions comes from how crops are grown, how livestock are raised, how land is managed and how food moves through global supply chains – not just from energy use. Addressing these emissions requires action across many sectors, including agriculture and food production. Decarbonization strategies in the food system can include improving farming practices, managing land more efficiently, reducing emissions in processing and transport, and cutting food loss and waste, while supporting long-term environmental and economic resilience.
