### Abstract & Problem Formulation Modern high-mix, low-volume manufacturing demands instant reconfigurability without sacrificing deterministic cycle timing or worker safety. This engineering paper investigates the architectural convergence of AMRGrid technologies within enterprise production lines, deriving mathematical state transitions, packet scheduling heuristics, and empirical validation benchmarks across high-speed industrial facilities. ### Theoretical Derivations & Closed-Loop Stability Consider an interconnected manufacturing network modeled as a directed graph $\mathcal{G} = (\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{E})$, where vertices $\mathcal{V}$ represent cyber-physical workcells, PLC controllers, and automated guided vehicles, while edges $\mathcal{E}$ denote deterministic communication links. We formulate the multi-agent queueing minimization objective: $\min_{\mathbf{p} \in \Pi} \sum_{i \in \mathcal{V}} \left( \alpha \cdot \|\mathbf{q}_i(t)\|^2 + \beta \cdot \int_0^T \|\mathbf{e}_i(t)\|^2 dt \right)$ Subject to deterministic deadline constraints: $\tau_{dispatch}(k) + \tau_{transit}(k) + \tau_{exec}(k) \le \Delta_{cycle}, \quad \forall k \in \mathcal{K}$ During transient disturbances (e.g., optical sensor occlusion or sudden stockout), dynamic re-routing algorithms recalculate task distribution across adjacent workcells within $4.2\text{ ms}$, preventing cascading stoppages. ### Empirical Validation & Benchmark Results We validated our framework across an operational 12-station smart assembly testbed operating 24/7 over 90 days: 1. **Fieldbus Jitter Suppression**: Mean clock skew across 64 distributed PTP nodes remained below $420\text{ ns}$ with a standard deviation of $68\text{ ns}$. 2. **Dynamic Cobot Reaction Time**: End-to-end latency from laser curtain intrusion…

